bodyText:
Iowa lets a tenant end a lease before its term runs out only in these situations:
- (1) you materially fail to comply with the lease, or fail to comply with your duty to keep the unit fit in a way that materially affects health and safety, and don't fix it within seven days after the tenant's written notice; if substantially the same breach recurs within six months, the tenant may end the lease on seven days' written notice (Iowa Code § 562A.21(1));
- (2) you fail to deliver possession at the start of the term, and the tenant gives at least five days' written notice (Iowa Code § 562A.22(1)(a));
- (3) fire or casualty substantially impairs the tenant's enjoyment of the unit, and the tenant moves out and gives written notice within 14 days (Iowa Code § 562A.25(1)(a); `edu-casualty-ia`);
- (4) you unlawfully remove or exclude the tenant or willfully cut off electricity, gas, water or another essential service (Iowa Code § 562A.26; `edu-self-help-eviction-ia`);
- (5) you enter unlawfully, enter lawfully in an unreasonable manner, or make repeated demands for entry that unreasonably harass the tenant (Iowa Code § 562A.35(2)); and
- (6) the tenant enters military service or receives qualifying orders (Iowa Code § 29A.101A; `edu-servicemember-ia`).

A tenant can't end the lease for a condition the tenant, the tenant's family or a guest caused (Iowa Code § 562A.21(1)(c)). When the lease ends under (1) to (4), you must return all prepaid rent and the recoverable security deposit (Iowa Code § 562A.21(4), Iowa Code § 562A.22(1)(a), Iowa Code § 562A.25(2), Iowa Code § 562A.26).

Iowa has no early-exit statute for victims of domestic abuse (`edu-no-dv-termination-ia`), a tenant's death (`edu-no-tenant-death-rule-ia`), age, illness or a move into care (`edu-no-infirmity-termination-ia`), job loss or a home purchase. Iowa has no statute letting a tenant end a lease because you made a fraudulent misrepresentation to get them to sign; the general law of fraud and misrepresentation still applies to leases (Iowa Code § 562A.3), but what it allows is a matter of case law. No Iowa statute makes you forfeit rent or other money because your lease leaves out a required statement; the only forfeiture in the act is losing the right to keep any of the deposit if you miss the 30-day statement (Iowa Code § 562A.12(4)).

A lease may not take away exits (1) to (5), which are rights under Iowa's landlord-tenant act, or make the tenant pay a fee to use them (Iowa Code § 562A.11(1)(a)), except in a lease of a single-family home on land assessed as agricultural land in an unincorporated area. The servicemember exit is in a different chapter (Iowa Code § 29A.101A); the cautious course is to treat it as one a lease cannot take away either.

Outside them, a tenant who leaves early is in breach, and you must make reasonable efforts to re-rent (Iowa Code § 562A.29(3); `edu-abandonment-mitigation-ia`). A month-to-month or week-to-week tenancy ends on ordinary notice (`edu-end-of-term-ia`).

Manufactured home community lots are governed by Iowa Code ch. 562B, not covered here.
